{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"pudgy-jolly-guppy","creation_channel":"mcp","description":"## Problem\nFindings carry a `severity` string derived from impact alone (`severity_label(impact)` in\n`review_kernel/decide.py`), which does not order the same way as the authoritative\n`priority = validity x impact`. This misleads readers into treating severity as the gate\nsignal when it is not, and caused a real field misread (a finding read as INFO then MEDIUM\nacross identical runs when only impact drifted). This decision changes the Gerrit LLM-Review\ncomment format and touches two review gates, so it needs a recorded ADR before any of the\nsibling implementation tickets land.\n\n## Approach\nWrite `docs/adr/<next-number>-retire-severity-label.md` recording:\n- the problem (impact-only label vs. validity x impact priority; the two duplicated\n  vocabularies: kernel's critical/major/minor/none and findings.py's 5-level\n  critical/high/medium/low/info);\n- the decision: findings surface `priority` (numeric) + a `blocking`/`advisory` boolean tag as\n  the canonical signal everywhere a human or a gate reads a finding; `severity` is removed from\n  the finding shape entirely (BREAKING; documented in release-notes.md/CHANGELOG.md by the\n  cleanup ticket);\n- the migration sequence (rank aggregation by priority -> surface blocking/advisory in\n  review-bot and CLI -> migrate `fail_on_severity` to a priority threshold -> drop the\n  `severity` field), each landing as its own reviewed change;\n- consequences: DET-tier findings (secrets detector, bugfix-size gate) get fixed priority\n  constants since they bypass `pass3_decide`; `severity_label()` itself is kept as an internal\n  impact-bucketing helper for calibration/eval use, decoupled from the Finding shape.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] `docs/adr/<n>-retire-severity-label.md` exists, follows the project's existing ADR\n  template (see `docs/adr/0009-review-bot-pipe.md` for shape).\n- [ ] The ADR names the four sibling implementation tickets by id and their landing order.\n- [ ] The ADR states the BREAKING schema consequence and where it is recorded.\n- [ ] `git grep -n \"retire-severity\"` finds the new ADR file, confirming it is committed\n  under `docs/adr/`.\n\n## Testing\nDocs-only change, no code path exercised. Verification is inspection: the ADR file exists,\nis committed, and is referenced by its sibling tickets.","id":"dc2c-0bb5-4272-46f3","parent_id":"c4c8-317a-85ca-4b08","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"task","title":"ADR: retire severity label, adopt priority + blocking/advisory as canonical signal"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1788112342602308600,"uuid":"dc04f35d-4cee-4d13-9704-14c8bd9d7798"}